Let's look at the error marker again. It points to the declaration in `diamond.h`. Often, in Exercism C++ exercises, the signature is expected to be `std::vector<std::string> rows(char letter)`. If the tests are written to pass a `char`, then `std::string` will fail to compile because there's no implicit conversion from `char` to `std::string` in the function call context (though there is from string literal to `std::string`).

Wait, if the tests pass `'A'` (a char), then `rows('A')` would fail to compile if the function expects `std::string`.
If the tests pass `"A"` (a string literal), then `rows("A")` works with `std::string`.

