real (floating-point) number `x` is the quotient `(a^x)/(b^x)`". This 
corresponds to the method `exp(double exponent)` in the class `Rational`. 
Usually `exp` implies $e^x$, but here it seems to be an instance method acting 
as $r^{exponent}$. Let's re-read carefully: "Exponentiation of a rational number
`r = a/b` to a real (floating-point) number `x` is the quotient `(a^x)/(b^x)`". 
Yes, so `rational.exp(x)` computes $r^x$.

8.  The instruction also mentions "Exponentiation of a real number `x` to a 
rational number `r = a/b` is `x^(a/b)`". This is typically a static method or a 
method on the real number class, but since we are only editing `Rational.java`, 
and there is no `pow` method taking a `double` base and returning a `double` in 
the provided skeleton (only `pow(int)` and `exp(double)`), I will assume this 
specific operation might be handled elsewhere or isn't required as a method on 
`Rational` itself based on the provided skeleton. The skeleton has `exp(double 
exponent)` which fits "rational to real power". It does not have a method for 
"real to rational power". I will stick to implementing the methods present in 
the skeleton.



